## State Handling - Loading, Empty, Error, and Success

Always explicitly handle all possible states when working with async data: **loading**, **empty**, **error**, and **success/content**.

## Core Principle

**Every component that fetches or displays data must handle all four states:**
1. **Loading** - Data is being fetched
2. **Empty** - Data fetch succeeded but no data exists
3. **Error** - Data fetch failed or operation errored
4. **Success/Content** - Data loaded successfully and should be displayed

## Convex Query States

For Convex queries (`useQuery`), the return value follows this pattern:

```typescript
const data = useQuery(api.myFunction.myQuery, args)
// data can be:
// - undefined (loading)
// - null (loaded but no data)
// - T (loaded with data)
// - throws error (error state - handled by error boundary)
```

### ❌ BAD: Missing State Handling

```tsx
function MyComponent() {
  const data = useQuery(api.myFunction.getData, { userId })
  
  // ❌ Assumes data exists - will crash if undefined or null
  return <div>{data.name}</div>
}
```

### ✅ GOOD: Complete State Handling (Manual)

```tsx
function MyComponent() {
  const data = useQuery(api.myFunction.getData, { userId })
  
  // Loading state
  if (data === undefined) {
    return (
      <div className="flex items-center justify-center min-h-screen">
        <div className="h-8 w-8 animate-spin rounded-full border-2 border-gray-300 border-t-gray-900" />
      </div>
    )
  }
  
  // Empty state
  if (data === null || (Array.isArray(data) && data.length === 0)) {
    return (
      <div className="text-center py-12">
        <p className="text-muted-foreground">No data available</p>
      </div>
    )
  }
  
  // Success/Content state
  return <div>{data.name}</div>
}
```

### ✅ BETTER: Using Custom Adapters

```tsx
import { useConvexQuery } from '@/hooks/useConvexQuery'
import { LoadingSpinner, EmptyState } from '@/components/ui/state-handlers'

function MyComponent() {
  const { data, isLoading, isEmpty } = useConvexQuery(
    api.myFunction.getData,
    { userId }
  )
  
  if (isLoading) return <LoadingSpinner />
  if (isEmpty) return <EmptyState message="No data available" />
  
  return <div>{data.name}</div>
}
```

## Convex Mutation States

For mutations (`useMutation`), handle errors explicitly:

### ❌ BAD: No Error Handling

```tsx
function MyComponent() {
  const createItem = useMutation(api.myFunction.createItem)
  
  const handleCreate = async () => {
    // ❌ No error handling
    await createItem({ name: 'Test' })
  }
  
  return <Button onClick={handleCreate}>Create</Button>
}
```

### ✅ GOOD: Error Handling (Manual)

```tsx
function MyComponent() {
  const createItem = useMutation(api.myFunction.createItem)
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null)
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false)
  
  const handleCreate = async () => {
    setError(null)
    setIsLoading(true)
    
    try {
      await createItem({ name: 'Test' })
      // Success - mutation completed
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err instanceof Error ? err.message : 'Failed to create item')
    } finally {
      setIsLoading(false)
    }
  }
  
  return (
    <div>
      {error && (
        <div className="text-red-500 mb-4">{error}</div>
      )}
      <Button onClick={handleCreate} disabled={isLoading}>
        {isLoading ? 'Creating...' : 'Create'}
      </Button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

### ✅ BETTER: Using Custom Adapters

```tsx
import { useConvexMutation } from '@/hooks/useConvexMutation'
import { ErrorDisplay } from '@/components/ui/state-handlers'

function MyComponent() {
  const { mutate, isLoading, error, resetError } = useConvexMutation(
    api.myFunction.createItem,
    {
      onSuccess: () => {
        // Handle success
      }
    }
  )
  
  return (
    <div>
      {error && <ErrorDisplay error={error} onDismiss={resetError} />}
      <Button onClick={() => mutate({ name: 'Test' })} disabled={isLoading}>
        {isLoading ? 'Creating...' : 'Create'}
      </Button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Multiple Queries Pattern

When using multiple queries, handle each one's loading state:

### ❌ BAD: Partial State Handling

```tsx
function Dashboard() {
  const mesocycle = useQuery(api.mesocycles.getActiveMesocycle, { userId })
  const workouts = useQuery(api.workouts.getRecentWorkouts, { userId })
  
  // ❌ Only checks mesocycle, workouts might be undefined
  if (mesocycle === undefined) {
    return <LoadingSpinner />
  }
  
  return <div>{mesocycle.name} - {workouts.length} workouts</div>
}
```

### ✅ GOOD: Complete State Handling (Manual)

```tsx
function Dashboard() {
  const mesocycle = useQuery(api.mesocycles.getActiveMesocycle, { userId })
  const workouts = useQuery(api.workouts.getRecentWorkouts, { userId })
  
  // Loading state - any query loading
  if (mesocycle === undefined || workouts === undefined) {
    return <LoadingSpinner />
  }
  
  // Empty state - check each query
  if (!mesocycle) {
    return <EmptyState message="No active mesocycle" />
  }
  
  // Success state - all data loaded
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{mesocycle.name}</h1>
      <p>{workouts.length} recent workouts</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

### ✅ BETTER: Using Custom Adapters

```tsx
import { useConvexQuery, useQueriesLoading } from '@/hooks/useConvexQuery'
import { LoadingSpinner, EmptyState } from '@/components/ui/state-handlers'

function Dashboard() {
  const mesocycle = useConvexQuery(api.mesocycles.getActiveMesocycle, { userId })
  const workouts = useConvexQuery(api.workouts.getRecentWorkouts, { userId })
  
  // Loading state - any query loading
  if (useQueriesLoading(mesocycle.data, workouts.data)) {
    return <LoadingSpinner />
  }
  
  // Empty state - check each query
  if (mesocycle.isEmpty) {
    return <EmptyState message="No active mesocycle" />
  }
  
  // Success state - all data loaded
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{mesocycle.data.name}</h1>
      <p>{workouts.data.length} recent workouts</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Conditional Queries Pattern

For queries that use `"skip"`, handle the skip state:

### ✅ GOOD: Conditional Query Handling

```tsx
function Component({ mesocycleId }: { mesocycleId: string | null }) {
  const mesocycle = useQuery(
    api.mesocycles.getMesocycleById,
    mesocycleId ? { id: mesocycleId } : "skip"
  )
  
  // Loading state
  if (mesocycleId && mesocycle === undefined) {
    return <LoadingSpinner />
  }
  
  // Empty/not found state
  if (mesocycleId && mesocycle === null) {
    return <EmptyState message="Mesocycle not found" />
  }
  
  // No ID provided
  if (!mesocycleId) {
    return <EmptyState message="Please select a mesocycle" />
  }
  
  // Success state
  return <div>{mesocycle.name}</div>
}
```

## Array Data Pattern

For array data, check both loading and empty array:

### ✅ GOOD: Array State Handling

```tsx
function WorkoutList() {
  const workouts = useQuery(api.workouts.getAllWorkouts, { userId })
  
  // Loading
  if (workouts === undefined) {
    return <LoadingSpinner />
  }
  
  // Empty array
  if (workouts.length === 0) {
    return (
      <div className="text-center py-12">
        <p className="text-muted-foreground">No workouts yet</p>
        <Button>Start First Workout</Button>
      </div>
    )
  }
  
  // Success - has items
  return (
    <ul>
      {workouts.map(workout => (
        <li key={workout._id}>{workout.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

## Error Boundaries

For query errors, use React Error Boundaries at route level:

```tsx
// Route component with error boundary
function MyRoute() {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<ErrorFallback />}>
      <MyComponent />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  )
}
```

## State Checklist

When creating a component with data fetching, ensure:

- [ ] **Loading state**: Show spinner/skeleton when `data === undefined`
- [ ] **Empty state**: Show empty message when `data === null` or `data.length === 0`
- [ ] **Error state**: Handle errors from mutations with try/catch
- [ ] **Success state**: Render content when data is available
- [ ] **Multiple queries**: Handle loading state for all queries
- [ ] **Conditional queries**: Handle skip state appropriately
- [ ] **User feedback**: Show loading indicators, error messages, empty states

## Common Patterns

### Loading Spinner Component

```tsx
function LoadingSpinner() {
  return (
    <div className="flex items-center justify-center min-h-screen">
      <div className="h-8 w-8 animate-spin rounded-full border-2 border-gray-300 border-t-gray-900" />
    </div>
  )
}
```

### Empty State Component

```tsx
function EmptyState({ message, action }: { message: string; action?: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <div className="text-center py-12">
      <p className="text-muted-foreground mb-4">{message}</p>
      {action}
    </div>
  )
}
```

### Error Display Component

```tsx
function ErrorDisplay({ error }: { error: string }) {
  return (
    <div className="rounded-md bg-red-50 p-4 text-red-800">
      <p>{error}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

